WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.040
Language is left-dominant in most people.

2
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:12.180
Yet in one study of 326 healthy adults, right-hemisphere dominance appeared in about

3
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.060
1 strong left-hander in 4.

4
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.840
Does that make most left-handers right-dominant?

5
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.380
It does not. To see why, we need the base rate.

6
00:00:21.383 --> 00:00:22.923
How was dominance measured?

7
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:26.523
With ultrasound over each temple, while people generated words.

8
00:00:27.143 --> 00:00:29.523
This is called functional transcranial Doppler.

9
00:00:30.023 --> 00:00:33.123
The side whose blood flow rises more is scored as dominant.

10
00:00:33.923 --> 00:00:38.743
Right-hemisphere dominance appeared in 4% % of strong right-handers, 15% % of

11
00:00:38.743 --> 00:00:42.753
ambidextrous people, and 27% % of strong left-handers.

12
00:00:42.753 --> 00:00:46.143
Left-handedness raises the odds of right-sided language.

13
00:00:46.643 --> 00:00:47.643
It does not make it typical.

14
00:00:48.717 --> 00:00:52.697
Now an exercise. Use the study's rates as fixed probabilities.

15
00:00:53.377 --> 00:00:57.697
Think of each person as one dot, blue for right-handers and yellow for left-handers.

16
00:00:58.257 --> 00:01:01.737
Take 100 strong right-handers and 100 strong left-handers.

17
00:01:02.297 --> 00:01:04.997
These group sizes are assumptions, not measured.

18
00:01:05.697 --> 00:01:09.957
Expect 4 of the right-handers to be right-dominant, and 27 of the left-handers.

19
00:01:09.957 --> 00:01:12.777
That makes 31 right-dominant people in all.

20
00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.630
Now pick one right-dominant person at random.

21
00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:19.990
This question runs backward from the one we started with.

22
00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:23.780
How likely is it that this person is left-handed?

23
00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.010
27 of the 31 are. That is 87%.

24
00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:30.650
Yet only 27% of left-handers are right-dominant.

25
00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:32.770
These are two different questions.

26
00:01:33.883 --> 00:01:35.803
Now change only the population.

27
00:01:36.363 --> 00:01:40.403
Make it 90% percent strong right-handers and 10% percent strong left-handers.

28
00:01:40.883 --> 00:01:42.043
The rates stay the same.

29
00:01:42.643 --> 00:01:45.843
Each group contributes its share of the population times its rate.

30
00:01:46.523 --> 00:01:48.423
Right-handers contribute 0.036.

31
00:01:48.423 --> 00:01:51.583
Left-handers contribute 0.027.

32
00:01:52.543 --> 00:01:56.523
Together, right-dominant people are 6.3% of the population.

33
00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:59.417
Pick a right-dominant person again.

34
00:01:59.937 --> 00:02:04.317
The chance of being left-handed is now 0.027 over 0.063.

35
00:02:04.637 --> 00:02:05.637
That is 43%. percent.

36
00:02:06.177 --> 00:02:07.237
The rates did not change.

37
00:02:07.777 --> 00:02:11.497
The base rate did, and the answer fell from 87 to 43 percent.

38
00:02:12.177 --> 00:02:15.737
Right-sided language is now more often found in a right-hander, simply because

39
00:02:15.737 --> 00:02:17.057
right-handers are so many.

40
00:02:17.717 --> 00:02:21.537
Left-handedness shifts the odds of right-sided language without reversing them.

41
00:02:22.177 --> 00:02:24.617
Same rates, different base rate, different answer.
